[{:seon.task/name "web-server-threads-are-joined-at-stop"
  :seon.task/plan "When the web proc binds its port, ring-chez-adapter's :fibers strategy runs its acceptor and its connection sweeper as two futures (two OS threads of Jolt's agent pool, clojure-agent-pool; the adapter's `start-sweeper!` and its acceptors in src/ring_chez/adapter.clj). The acceptor polls accept() in accept-poll-ms slices; the sweeper is (Thread/sleep 100) in a loop, a clock (AGENTS §4). stop-server waits for the acceptor but only flags the sweeper, so after `seon.root/stop!` the sweeper outlives its owner for up to 100 ms: root's thread count after stop! depends on timing, so bin/check lifecycle runs with SEON_WEB_PORT=0. The adapter's :keep-alive-timeout-ms 600000 and :write-timeout-ms 60000 (`seon.web/web-proc`) are application timers and literals (docs/clocks.md#time-decides-owed-its-owners-fix). Correct: the listener is a fiber parked on the listen fd's readiness (jolt.io-poller); an idle connection parks on its own read (a fact, not a sweep); a stuck write is the peer's half-open socket, the kernel's keepalive (the owner's exception); the web proc's ::flow/stop returns after both are gone. Then `seon.check/declared-threads` drops its \"clojure-agent-pool\" 2 for a listening web proc, and lifecycle runs with the web on. Deletes: start-sweeper!, the accept poll slice, the two adapter timeouts, the SEON_WEB_PORT=0 in bin/check's lifecycle step. A dependency change: on a jolt/<topic> branch of the adapter. Read 2026-11-06: `seon.web/web-proc` still passes the two timeouts, and `seon.check/declared-threads` still declares \"clojure-agent-pool\" 2. The proof named below is green on main with that declaration, so it shows the change only once that line goes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tabs-first-keyframe-settles-on-its-own"
  :seon.task/plan "Two symptoms of bin/check idle waiting with no end, one occurrence each so far. (1) 2026-11-08: the tab's first keyframe and settled mark never came (below). (2) 2026-11-10: the tab was past its keyframe and both settled marks, after the check's spawn, and waited for the bytes its last change should send. The check's commit ({:ns/name idle.check}) is nothing root's page reads: root's pull covers no namespace entity, root's own writes send no event (so root's process tile, the writer's commit count, is not read again), and seon.render-test/a-commit-root-does-not-show-owes-its-tab-nothing shows such a commit sends the tab nothing. So commit-reached-tab ended only on bytes something else happened to send (the new agent's status moving), and under load those landed before its count was read. Fixed in the check (Web, 2026-11-10): it commits a held task, which root's head counts (seon.render-test/a-commit-root-shows-reaches-a-quiet-root-tab: the tab is sent a patch). seon.render-test/a-spawn-reaches-a-quiet-root-tab shows the spawn wait is owed too, and seon.render.proc/process! already marks :root dirty when root's process value moves. Evidence for (2): ps at the checkpoint puts the agent's fork about 4 s after boot (the check's own spawn, after the two settled waits and the 2,000 ms window), lsof shows the check's feed socket open; the fiber dumps (all parked, none naming its proc) are in the managing session's notes folder, idle-hang-2026-11-10/. bin/check idle now prints \"IDLE waiting: <name>\" before each of its five waits, so the next occurrence names its wait. Symptom (1)'s cause stays open: read from that line when it recurs. Noted, not for this task: root's process value is a second path into root's ratom beside its pull (the owner's option a), so a number in it that moves with no event (the writer's commits on root's own writes) shows stale; that is decided at stage 3/4.

Seen 2026-11-08 in bin/check idle (a land of jolt/rootagent with main's af549ba258 and f9bda5bd88 merged): the check's tab opened the root view's feed and waited about 30 minutes for its \": settled\" mark; the check's root was idle (every fiber parked on core.async alts, no CPU). A second request to the same feed (curl /feed?view=root) got its keyframe at once, and within seconds the check's own tab settled and the step ended IDLE OK. So a tab's first keyframe or settled mark can wait on another tab opening the same view: the web proc's open of a tab (seon.web http-in :open: :views to the render proc, then :keyframe) or the render proc's answer to it loses a keyframe asked while another is in flight, or answers only a view's newest tab. Evidence: the Web session's scratchpad idle-hang-40858 (fiber dump, sample, lsof) and the run's target/check output. Find the cause before fixing. Read so far (Web, 2026-11-08): a tab is stale until a keyframe reaches it (seon.web keyframe!, to every stale tab of its view), and a settled mark goes only to fresh tabs (offer-all skips stale ones), so a tab whose keyframe never comes never settles: the second tab's keyframe reached the check's stale tab too, which is why it unstuck. The render proc answers a keyframe at once when the view is ready? (rendered; a branch read), else holds it in :kf-wanted and asks root's state, sending it from the pass an answer brings; a pass runs only on an input, so a wanted keyframe whose answer's pass still finds its view not ready, with nothing else in flight, waits for ever. Ruled out: a nil keyframe (view-html composes a string skeleton; compose refuses nil by contract). Candidate cause, read 2026-11-08 night (seon.render.proc answered): an error answer to :state (or to :main) is taken and nothing more (\"the next event asks again\"), so a keyframe in :kf-wanted or a page in :waiting waits for the next event, which an idle root never has: a wait whose producer answered an error, with nothing to end it (AGENTS 4: every wait ends, with its value or a typed error). Not shown to be this hang's: the check's log has no defect line for it. Fixed on jolt/findings (seon.render.proc answered; proof seon.render-test/a-wanted-keyframe-is-answered-when-its-read-fails, red then green): an error answer to :state or :main holds each waiting view's page as the error, drawn, which answers its keyframe and page and walks again at every pass until a read draws it. If the hang recurs with no error answer, the cause is elsewhere: read the pass after :state's answer below. The class fix as planned was: on an error answer, every page waiting and keyframe wanted is answered with that error drawn (a page :status 503 by the error's render; a keyframe that draws it, so the tab turns fresh and the next commit redraws it), and the proof is a keyframe asked while :state answers an error is answered. Next otherwise: the pass after :state's answer for the root view (root-page is nil until main is read: is main's read asked, and does its answer's pass send :kf-wanted?), and keyframe! closing a tab whose offer! is refused.

Proofs, red first: a tab that opens a view while that view's keyframe for an earlier tab is in flight gets its own keyframe and settled mark, with no other tab opening."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.web-test/each-tab-gets-its-own-keyframe}}]
